ManicksGalaxy — Complete Product Requirements Document (PRD v0.2)
29 October 2026 · Complete working draft

This document integrates the scientific/product foundation from v0.1 with a new end-to-end UX blueprint and sitemap. It is a proposed design, not a claim that the described software already exists.
Part I — Core Product, Science and Engineering
Version: 0.1 — Discovery draft
Date: 29 October 2026
Working category: Interactive astronomy encyclopedia and 3D space exploration platform
Audience: Children and schools, families, curious adults, astronomy enthusiasts
Status: Proposal; technical and rights reviews required before implementation
1. Executive summary
ManicksGalaxy is an immersive, browser-based astronomy encyclopedia: a navigable 3D solar system, scientifically grounded night sky, a curated galaxy and nebula explorer, and learning journeys for different ages. People should be able to look, orbit, travel, compare and learn, not simply read entries or watch canned videos.

North star: From a question about an object in the universe to a compelling visual explanation in fewer than three meaningful interactions.

Product promise: "Explore the universe from every angle." The experience will distinguish observed imagery, data-based simulation, and artist's impression. Not all 360-degree views can be genuine photography, and the product must be honest about reconstructions.
2. Product goals and non-goals
Goals
1. Make astronomy engaging without sacrificing factual accuracy.
2. Offer controllable 360° object inspection with smooth, predictable camera movements.
3. Map celestial objects to sourced encyclopedia entries, animations and curriculum-friendly explanations.
4. Enable meaningful learning: scales, orbits, gravity, seasons, light, star patterns and deep space.
5. Make the experience practical on school laptops and capable mobile devices.
6. Store provenance and asset usage rights as first-class product data.
Non-goals for MVP
- Recreating every star and galaxy in the observable universe as individually accurate 3D meshes.
- Claiming that rendered exoplanet or galaxy surfaces are photographs when they are not.
- A real-time multiplayer universe, full VR headset support, realistic spacecraft piloting or entire-surface photogrammetry for all planets.
- Unrestricted scraping or republishing of third-party models or videos.
- Personalised accounts for children in the initial release.
3. Target audiences and user jobs
Audience	What they need	First release support
Ages approximately 7–12	Visually guided, simple factual answers	Explore/Discover modes; narrated and text summaries; glossary; quizzes
Ages 13–17	More scientific detail, controls and comparisons	Orbit controls, time scrubber, numeric data, concepts and diagrams
Teachers	Reusable demonstrations and short lessons	Shareable links to a preset scene, classroom-friendly mode, source credits
General audience	Beautiful immersive learning	Guided journeys, searchable encyclopedia, favourites on device
