4. Experience architecture
4.1 Main routes / areas
- / — Cinematic entry, search, "Continue Exploring", featured journeys.
- /explore/solar-system — Solar-system map with all eight planets, the Sun, selected moons, dwarf planets and key satellites.
- /object/[slug] — Unified celestial object experience: 3D scene, facts, highlights, media, references, learning challenges.
- /sky — Earth-observer night sky by optional location, date, time and direction; constellation overlays.
- /constellations — 88 IAU-defined regions; educational line drawings clearly labelled as conventional illustrations.
- /galaxies — Milky Way, selected galaxies and nebulae; 2D observation plus 3D interpretation when defensible.
- /exoplanets — Searchable catalogue plus curated artist impressions; sizes, discovery methods, host star and uncertainties.
- /journeys — Narrated or text-guided camera experiences such as "Journey to Saturn", "Earth to the Moon", "Through the Orion Nebula".
- /learn — Topic collections, quizzes, glossary and teacher resources.
- /sources — Licenses, attribution, data sources, correction history.
4.2 Core screen structure
1. Immersive viewport — 3D canvas or telescope sky viewer; overlay controls do not obscure the focal object.
2. Object card — Name, type, key measurements, one-sentence hook, science/artist-impression badge.
3. Camera tray — Orbit, pan, zoom, fly, reset, auto-tour, full screen and orientation indicator.
4. Timeline — Pause, play, rate control and date/time selection for orbiting/sky scenes.
5. Knowledge drawer — Overview, structure, composition, discoveries, missions, comparisons, sources.
6. Learning layer — Guided questions, age/depth toggle, unit choice and optional narration.
5. Camera and 360° interaction requirements
Important terminology: A 360° object view is an orbitable 3D model; a 360° panorama is a photograph or rendering captured from a fixed observation point; a cinematic video is not interactively orbitable.
Camera mode	User action	Expected behaviour
Orbit	Drag or swipe around planet	Change yaw/pitch freely; maintain an intelligible focal point
Inspect	Scroll/pinch zoom, touch controls	Move between wide planetary view and closer visual detail without clipping
Fly-to	Select a planet, moon or star	Smooth camera path easing into the destination; allow skip and cancel
Compare	Select two objects	Side-by-side objects; optional same-scale and educational-scale views
Surface	Choose available landing site or panorama	360° panoramic look-around when real imagery exists; otherwise label reconstruction
Sky observer	Change observer location/time and orientation	Render accurate apparent sky, constellation boundaries and selected object tracks
Cinematic	Start guided journey	Scripted camera moves with narration/overlays and user skip controls
Advanced	Choose view from above orbit plane/pole/equator	Change framing, axial tilt, illuminated phase and relevant measurements


Required controls: Pointer drag, keyboard navigation and zoom, touch swipe and pinch, reset view, pause motion, reduced-motion setting, full-screen option, visible scale mode and camera origin. Camera moves must never trap the user in a noninterruptible animation.

Scale and coordinate rules: Use explicit coordinate frames (e.g., heliocentric ecliptic vs ICRS), epoch/time, unit conversions and interpolation methodology. Implement camera-relative/floating-origin or hierarchical scene scales because ordinary GPU precision is insufficient for one literal 3D coordinate system spanning planetary to galactic distances. Explain when scale or orbital speeds are exaggerated for learning.
6. Content inventory for MVP
- 1 Sun, 8 planets, Earth’s Moon, a starter collection of major moons, 5 recognized dwarf planets as available.
- 88 IAU constellation entries, with basic information and carefully sourced star map overlays; start with 10 richly narrated featured constellations.
- 20–30 curated nebulae, galaxies and star clusters, primarily telescope-image-led with optional labelled visualization.
- 20–30 curated exoplanets, with real NASA Exoplanet Archive data and clearly labelled illustrations where needed.
- 10 introductory guided journeys and 25–40 short lesson modules (final scope dependent on the team and curriculum mapping).
Each object record includes name, aliases, category, measurements with units, uncertainty where meaningful, observation references, image credits, 3D asset provenance, classification of visual authenticity and reading levels.
7. Visual style and animation system
Art direction
- Dark, believable space with restrained volumetric light, accurate lighting direction and physically plausible planetary terminators.
- High-detail planet textures, cloud/atmosphere layers where available, ring transparency for Saturn, moon crater detail where supported.
- Intentional cinematic composition, smooth camera easing, very readable text and subdued interface chrome.
- Choice between Science mode (accurate labels, explicit scale and sources) and Explore mode (dramatic transitions, educational exaggerations labelled).
Visual authenticity tiers
- OBSERVED: Image captured by telescope/spacecraft (may involve processing or composite wavelengths).
- DATA-BASED: Simulation using observed orbital/catalogue data or remote-sensing textures.
- RECONSTRUCTED: Artist's plausible approximation from partial data.
- ARTISTIC: Interpretive scene not presented as scientifically proven visual truth.
These labels must be visible on scenes and object metadata, including exoplanets, galaxy interiors, black holes and unobserved planetary regions.
Engine separation
- Motion (Framer Motion successor): Navigation, panel choreography, typography, loading transitions, quizzes and reduced-motion-aware UI.
- Three.js + React Three Fiber + Drei: Real-time meshes, shaders, atmospheric effects, particle fields, orbiting objects and camera navigation.
- Blender: Convert/edit models, create normals and LODs, bake details and export GLB.
- Video: NASA SVS and other properly licensed MP4/WebM materials for cinematic cutaways and background journeys, never as a substitute for 360° interactive inspection.
- Sky atlas: Aladin Lite embeds for real survey imagery; independently constructed constellations from catalogue coordinates where required.
