9. Asset-ingestion pipeline
Workflow: Source discovery → permitted retrieval → license + author verification → asset manifest → integrity/antivirus scan → scientific/editing review → processing → previews → compressed CDN upload → attribution display → scheduled freshness review.

Asset manifest (minimum): asset_id, object_id, title, kind, source_url, download_url_or_source_id, creator, credit_line, license_id, license_url, reuse_scope, authenticity_tier, capture_date_or_data_epoch, reviewer, rights_review_date, checksum_sha256, original_format, optimized_format, bytes, lod_level, attribution_required, last_verified, status.

Preferred formats: .glb meshes, .ktx2 compressed textures, .webp/`.avif` images, .mp4/`.webm` cinematic video, processed binary star point sets and JSON metadata. Retain unmodified master files separately where license permits. Use source-specific attribution, e.g. "NASA/JPL-Caltech" rather than assuming generic "NASA" always suffices.

Scraping policy: Only use official APIs, explicit open data exports, permitted bulk archives, or downloads provided by source owners. Check terms, attribution, robots directives where relevant, request rate limits, and hotlink policies. Do not capture another site's 3D viewer or copyrighted experience and repackage it as your own.
10. Proposed technical architecture
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  S[Official astronomy data + media] --> I[Licensed ingestion & processing]
  I --> D[(Catalog DB + provenance)]
  I --> O[(CDN object storage: GLB KTX2 images videos)]
  D --> A[Content / astronomy APIs]
  A --> W[React/Next.js application]
  O --> W
  W --> U[Motion UI + knowledge panels]
  W --> T[React Three Fiber real-time universe]
  W --> K[Aladin Lite real-sky survey view]
  J[JPL ephemeris / cached science engine] --> A
```
Suggested baseline: React + Next.js + TypeScript + Tailwind; Three.js + @react-three/fiber + @react-three/drei; motion for UI; Zustand or equivalent lightweight scene state; PostgreSQL for structured content and citations; CDN/object storage for heavy media; offline scientific ingestion scripts, with backend caching. Avoid rendering large GPU canvases on the server; use progressive/client-side loading. Version all scientific data products and assets.

Existing uploaded project opportunities: Rashinos already has React/Next and an astronomy-engine dependency, along with orbital visuals and JPL Horizons validation logic. Reuse validated mathematical and UI engineering patterns after code review; do not reuse astrological interpretation as astronomy lessons. PlyQuest, MusikFlo and PromptLaZ share React/Motion patterns that can inform consistent interface transitions. A new 3D renderer is still required; decorative SVG starfields are not a scientific spatial catalogue.
11. Non-functional requirements
Performance
- Target smooth interactivity at ~60 fps on supported desktops and ~30 fps on mid-range mobile, with scene-specific tests rather than a guarantee on all devices.
- Progressive loading: low-res preview first; lazy-load higher-res textures, models and star tiles; cull invisible content and set device-quality tiers.
- Limit simultaneous particle count, texture memory and shader complexity; avoid shipping 8K textures to every device.
- Keep accurate positions independent of visual rendering speed; camera motion must not drift with frame rate.
Accessibility
- WCAG-oriented UI, keyboard-operable controls, readable labels, captioned narration, high-contrast mode and reduced-motion support.
- Fully readable text/facts fallback for devices unable to run WebGL; no learning objective may depend solely on animation or color.
Trust, science and privacy
- Editorial review for major facts and source dates; label uncertain quantities and approximations.
- No account required to explore; no precise-location collection without explicit choice; child-appropriate defaults; legal review before school accounts or telemetry for children.
- No implied NASA/ESA endorsement. Show visible credits and license terms for individual assets.
12. User stories and acceptance criteria
US-01: Visit Saturn
As a learner, I can select Saturn and orbit it so I can examine its rings from above, the side and below.
Acceptance: Saturn loads in a controllable 3D scene; orbit supports a complete horizontal revolution, adjustable vertical camera angle, pinch/scroll zoom, reset and pause; the model displays credited textures, ring inclination, and basic facts; performance fallback works.
US-02: Discover Orion
As a student, I can look at Orion in an observer sky and see which stars form the familiar pattern.
Acceptance: Star positions derive from documented data; a recognisable line-art convention is disclosed; IAU region is distinct from the depicted asterism; significant stars have short descriptions; date/time change affects the apparent sky.
US-03: Compare Earth with Jupiter
As a child, I can compare the planets' sizes and learn why Jupiter is so much larger.
Acceptance: Two representations have selectable same-scale and presentation-scale modes; numerical diameters have clear units; exaggeration is explicitly labelled; UI works by touch and keyboard.
US-04: Navigate from Earth to a galaxy
As a visitor, I can start a guided journey that zooms out from Earth through cosmic scales.
Acceptance: Transition is interruptible, scene changes preserve clarity, distance units update, scale discontinuities are explained, and reconstructions are labelled.
US-05: Verify an asset's origin
As a teacher, I can find a source and media credit for every factual object and major visualization.
Acceptance: Source panel contains accessible credits, original link, license and authenticity type; unreviewed assets are blocked from publishing.
13. Phased delivery
Phase 0 — technical proof: One photorealistic Saturn scene with rings, camera rig, atmospheric/lighting prototype, mobile performance measurements and complete rights manifest. One Jupiter/Earth compare view and one constellation prototype.

Phase 1 — usable MVP: Solar system, core encyclopedia, search, 88 constellation entries (featured subset with richer narration), 10 guided journeys, basic quizzes, credits, mobile accessibility, telemetry limited to privacy-safe site quality.

Phase 2 — deep-space expansion: Richer galaxies, interactive sky surveys, curated exoplanets, more moon flyovers, educator sharing, more languages and robust editorial tooling.

Phase 3 — advanced immersion: Optional WebXR support, high-end volumetric and galaxy shaders, spatial audio, collaborative lessons, AR modes, and larger scientific star catalogs after profiling.
14. KPIs and quality signals
- Percentage of visitors who successfully orbit an object and open its knowledge panel.
- Median time to a first interactive, educational 3D view; % of slow-device fallbacks working.
- Number of guided journeys completed and quiz comprehension improvements, evaluated responsibly.
- Broken media/licensing audit findings (target zero unresolved before publish).
- Accuracy incidents and time-to-correction; credit completeness; accessibility defects.
- Performance distributions by device class, not only synthetic desktop benchmarks.
15. Top risks and mitigations
Risk	Mitigation
"Photorealistic" mistaken for "photographed"	Mandatory visual authenticity tags and source panels
Unusable download/redistribution licenses	Pre-publish legal check, per-asset manifest, permitted sources first
Mobile GPU overload	Device tiers, texture compression, LODs, fallback content
Huge differences in astronomical scale	Hierarchical coordinate frames, floating origins, labelled scale switches
